@page "/docs/accessibility/audit-builds"

@* izzy audit on a build server: --fail-on any|real|new and exit code 5, --baseline (AuditVerdicts.cs), the browser,
   a key for the account (docs/tools/account), results.sarif for code scanning (AuditTrace.cs). *@

<ArticlePage Section="accessibility" Slug="audit-builds">
    <Lead>
        Run <Code>izzy audit</Code> in a build to catch a problem when it is added, not months later. The build
        can fail on any finding, on real ones, or only on those a change brought in.
    </Lead>
    <ChildContent>
        <Heading Level="2">Fail a build on findings</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            With <Code>--fail-on any</Code>, izzy ends with exit code 5 when the page has a finding. When the
            page has none and a load didn't open or answered with an error, it ends with exit code 1. On a build server, start the app with
            <Code>--run</Code>:
        </TextBlock>
        <Code Block Language="bash">izzy audit / --run --fail-on any</Code>
        <TextBlock>
            The build server needs Chrome, Edge or Chromium. If izzy doesn't find one, set <Code>--browser</Code> or
            the <Code>IZZY_BROWSER</Code> variable to its path.
        </TextBlock>

        <Heading Level="2">Fail on real findings</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            With a free account, <Code>--fail-on real</Code> ends with exit code 5 only when a finding's
            <Anchor Href="docs/accessibility/audit-verdicts">verdict</Anchor> is real. False positives, demo pages
            and the findings you accepted in <Code>.izzy/audit.json</Code> don't fail the build. Neither do the
            findings that need a person: they are in the report and the task list for someone to look at.
        </TextBlock>
        <TextBlock>
            A build server signs in with a key in the <Code>IZZY_KEY</Code> variable: see
            <Anchor Href="docs/tools/account">Your account</Anchor>. A key gives a build what a free account
            has: one page a run. <Code>--site</Code> and <Code>--urls</Code> need a sign-in with Pro.
        </TextBlock>

        <Heading Level="2">Fail only on new findings</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            To stop new findings on a site that already has some, keep a past run's <Code>results.json</Code> in
            the repository and compare with it:
        </TextBlock>
        <Code Block Language="bash">izzy audit / --run --baseline audit-baseline.json --fail-on new</Code>
        <TextBlock>
            A real finding the baseline lacks is new, and only those fail the build. The report says how many are
            new and how many are fixed, and <Code>tasks.md</Code> checks off the fixed ones. Remake the baseline
            when the old findings are fixed.
        </TextBlock>

        <Heading Level="2">Code scanning</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            <Code>--format sarif</Code> writes <Code>results.sarif</Code>, which GitHub code scanning and editors
            read. It holds the real findings and those a person must look at, each on its line. A finding izzy
            couldn't place is on the first place it may be, or on line 1 of its page's file, with its selector in
            the message. A finding on a
            page that has no file in the app is left out, since code scanning takes no result without a place.
            It stays in the other reports. Paths are written from the repository's root. The format needs the
            app's source: add <Code>--run</Code> or <Code>--project</Code>.
        </TextBlock>
        <Code Block Language="bash">izzy audit /orders --run --format md,json,sarif</Code>

        <Heading Level="2">Exit codes</Heading>
        <Table Items="Codes" Caption="Exit codes of izzy audit" Size="ControlSize.Sm" Class="site-params" Bordered="false">
            <Columns>
                <TemplateColumn Title="Code" Width="Length.Rem(6)">@context!.Name</TemplateColumn>
                <TemplateColumn Title="Means">@context!.Text</TemplateColumn>
            </Columns>
        </Table>

        <Heading Level="2">What a green build means</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            A build that passes had no finding of the kind you chose, on the pages it audited, as they load. It
            doesn't check a dialog nobody opened, the order of the Tab key or what a screen reader says. With
            <Code>--fail-on real</Code> or <Code>new</Code>, a green build can still have findings that need a
            person and elements axe couldn't measure. They are in the report.
            Keep the checks <Anchor Href="docs/accessibility/testing">a person does</Anchor> in your release steps.
        </TextBlock>
    </ChildContent>
</ArticlePage>

@code {
    private sealed record Row(string Name, string Text);

    private static readonly Row[] Codes =
    [
        new("0", "The audit ran, and nothing --fail-on counts was found."),
        new("1", "No load of the page opened. With --fail-on, also when a load didn't open or answered with an error and nothing else failed the run. A recheck couldn't be made, or the command was wrong."),
        new("3", "The option needs IzzyBlazor Pro: --site."),
        new("4", "The option needs a free account, and izzy isn't signed in."),
        new("5", "Findings that --fail-on counts, or a rechecked finding that is still there and open."),
    ];
}
